    "knowledge_point": "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation",
    "problem": "Suppose that $f(4.9)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${f}^{′}(4.9)=5.$ Using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=4.9$, estimate the value of $f(5).$",
    "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=4.9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(4.9)+{f}^{′}(4.9)(x-4.9) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+5(x-4.9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(5)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & ≈L(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+5(5-4.9) \\\\  & =0.5+5(0.1) \\\\  & =0.5+0.5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
